magine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f352f1a-1af2-5144-a245-10854ead8b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446554} {:answer/id #uuid "41e8b7d6-5c7a-5cf8-9c06-4d00a18513e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446557} {:answer/id #uuid "834c390d-d089-5913-89c4-08a44764f21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446558} {:answer/id #uuid "dcefe323-f572-5c28-853b-0586c0615884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c44a28-6809-582c-b411-ffd64f99822a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcefe323-f572-5c28-853b-0586c0615884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc4e87e-69ab-505e-8ac2-a813c8707da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446571}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "322bc9a1-81a0-53ce-920d-1108825e3484" :question/math-academy-id "q-131363" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5ac051f640c2b4eae760eee9c567ec35ce3169384ec2305554e21c6d2d587bbe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nIV. $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c74d4b9-7ed4-529a-b818-13e80317f0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} {:answer/id #uuid "976ecb2e-4f04-5610-8207-c96b23772842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446561} {:answer/id #uuid "b06370cf-7aa1-54f8-9e46-dc3a42a8d19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446563} {:answer/id #uuid "c7df0803-65d5-5e82-a045-a191ec18b671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446564} {:answer/id #uuid "e214b4cf-1c30-5601-8ce0-ad80b4de52ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c74d4b9-7ed4-529a-b818-13e80317f0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667cd59-08f8-5c6e-82c5-8b3ea70d6405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40fa6adc-4679-5e13-a9fc-cb698f54e0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105700" :question/problem "![](images/18/183ea541b33b8389de46f9ee1560ed1da2b999191477d7490eb4970922bdc43c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $F$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5c788ad2c220b70a7f1d333680a70933f9b490a0db11fb3440dd3a2e06fa01.png)\n\nFind a definite integral that's equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm{d} x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(3,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0+t(3-0),\\,0+t(3-0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t+3t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512 :topic/math-academy-id 3699 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Line Segments"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512 :topic/math-academy-id 3688 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Circles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616 :topic/math-academy-id 3690 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Ellipses"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4d7dd5-663e-510d-8402-fe5e2a5d6823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27\\displaystyle \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos t\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130643} {:answer/id #uuid "d16a3cf8-b23d-556a-8199-cbd45cb82bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\displaystyle \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130644} {:answer/id #uuid "d794e981-be9d-57e3-9348-5f9d5cd1cc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81\\displaystyle \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130645} {:answer/id #uuid "f57da3ad-20ac-546c-b258-f0710c7e20f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\displaystyle \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130646} {:answer/id #uuid "1aaace32-eb57-5850-ba33-72cfb823554d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\displaystyle \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130647}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6d405a14-e5d7-5d6e-8a51-d8cbb6ee5709" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130642}] :question/id #uuid "03d13445-5c0b-517f-ac68-3ccc5d850fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124119" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e78048e3d84d43d9a1e0f846c431452eea5ebf974bd460e74c70fbc99c6cf1bd.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}x^{2}ydx$, where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3$, traversed from the point $(0, 3)$ to the point $(3, 0)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨3\\cos t, 3\\sin t⟩$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cde398-644e-5383-bd99-792099c41619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257117} {:answer/id #uuid "93dc2ce1-f21b-5c93-ab43-d2748bcf7e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258085} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6527f4-2b6d-5023-9738-faa5c0a3b742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258269} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ee4b47-07d9-52aa-939a-fa180de13ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258481} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a5d732-d338-5c3b-8b31-a61f71335d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93dc2ce1-f21b-5c93-ab43-d2748bcf7e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258085} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c063bef-91c3-595d-ab1b-3260d192fba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259272}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f523c211-f380-5764-9fbd-9f50f1b0e80c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124230" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01825306-ac24-5300-973f-a0c4b791365b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256796} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27a531-9de1-56f9-836a-e1736077898d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257349} {:answer/id #uuid "8e35d176-d483-5c6c-8219-0e38dad049fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} {:answer/id #uuid "9c536383-f7c3-5cfe-8d3b-1ffc7943f5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258157} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d64c61-750a-55b2-80ed-e2d9402be4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e35d176-d483-5c6c-8219-0e38dad049fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae531638-bc65-5f25-9c4c-6658f8c3500e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591574ce-dde0-5375-8d9f-24f9b5f86769" :question/math-academy-id "q-124223" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3,$ traversed from the point $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $(0,-\\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2168c8cd-5aeb-586f-8d06-cc8367809f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257056} {:answer/id #uuid "2b650bd1-0837-5f08-99d5-a807e4c27e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257170} {:answer/id #uuid "35123647-df55-5d75-89f2-0aca23bfb5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257249} {:answer/id #uuid "59c84fb3-3212-58ee-92d9-6c0807dbf87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2b2604-95aa-59ca-8d59-13a86f0b5357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59c84fb3-3212-58ee-92d9-6c0807dbf87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b84b78-85d8-5936-a4d9-bebaf84cdf71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a31726b8-8b33-50ff-9a55-ad044b065273" :question/math-academy-id "q-124234" :question/problem "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a914125-7099-5b0c-a1c1-9224eef57080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369260} {:answer/id #uuid "7213e0d0-98dc-5715-bb95-024708c2eadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369261} {:answer/id #uuid "9a304234-899b-5867-ae14-f933577257a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0a400c-fbad-505f-8344-98b05be346a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369263} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5940aa-094f-59da-a9fc-f4dda58c537a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a304234-899b-5867-ae14-f933577257a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc543956-16a6-524a-b735-fabf30c10bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369259}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55218946-84ed-5872-a1c7-f6fd7a57a20f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124226" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f402fe97e6f76cd94b94331e7f30ddb09c978c93b86ce112a5232042a2f741cc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66dc688f-81a1-5bab-892b-ab84541785d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387472} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cf7a39-05f3-5b46-9687-4304489da63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aaa33f-356e-5b31-9322-7fc460783b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387474} {:answer/id #uuid "6904468a-7356-52cb-8fc4-603b5ee7fd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387475} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bac43c-45b2-592b-a306-006e20a13c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cf7a39-05f3-5b46-9687-4304489da63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a48ec71-cabe-53f3-8125-9985126072d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76ca20f1-204f-59f9-8369-ed13b85fecd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124218" :question/problem "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b65a2499-03be-5905-9103-3bc639070075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387479} {:answer/id #uuid "02e5a48e-caff-5b56-923a-edebcab3b203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387480} {:answer/id #uuid "ef19f835-6146-5aac-b21a-93caea15dc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387481} {:answer/id #uuid "043ccaf9-23b9-56e1-8926-73f293a9c821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387482} {:answer/id #uuid "088e8adc-3efa-55dc-b823-271ee24d9d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088e8adc-3efa-55dc-b823-271ee24d9d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387483} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a794707-9bf7-53f4-9c51-8d9a3d67187e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6572425b-1f69-589f-ade1-f4702feda38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124221" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9babf4c2ec631b3b872760261a46c3552cb93b93df90515ff0605c15043841ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133714 :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d308c6-8a57-5de5-bcc7-bf40d867d0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130721} {:answer/id #uuid "adb3a2f4-608c-5138-8635-60f40d20bbb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130722} {:answer/id #uuid "b425488f-374f-5fbf-a20f-6ed75b115a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130723} {:answer/id #uuid "bda8a425-c21e-5b88-81a7-318e0cea26f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130724} {:answer/id #uuid "669852c3-9028-56fe-aab7-f1bce32786a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130725}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8fcbecb7-a504-5e86-b075-909351068095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130720}] :question/id #uuid "64f58b26-9afd-5961-840b-da865c629781" :question/math-academy-id "q-126427" :question/problem "Given that $F(x, y) = xyi + yj$, what is the scalar curl of $F$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613b73ad-b4bd-580f-91d8-dabafde0761e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409595} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf92fb5-00f1-572a-8207-d72045cf4a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409599} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} {:answer/id #uuid "ee35c795-c099-5c49-977b-052171747158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409603} {:answer/id #uuid "f7113576-7a9c-589a-960b-40c8d014ca07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} :answer-field/id #uuid "9898eac0-330a-51e3-bb68-1c7f9a46f935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409607}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126428" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc3ca35d-b2c9-5696-ab5b-faec203fe6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ca35d-b2c9-5696-ab5b-faec203fe6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234892} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a019d5-8e68-590c-aeae-b08b6bbb3998" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a254e595-25b7-5631-ae7f-2ec647939f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-168118" :question/problem "The scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $V$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13264dd3-32ca-5425-ae4a-10ae592d25f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260044} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4d041-01f8-5cba-bbc5-711d818eb654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692} {:answer/id #uuid "7344edf5-4fa2-53cc-aefd-2082b97c5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} {:answer/id #uuid "deb142df-e54d-5f60-a501-f62bc2ccccc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} :answer-field/id #uuid "6208b069-a1cc-5b39-96a3-5295dfbd12c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262291}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84980841-460b-507f-8790-439efc8ff63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad86e3ef-c12c-5d63-830a-dabac47cd90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261396} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96fb47-5ab0-591e-bc64-e12732e133be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261400} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd7a97a-dfe1-5328-9d66-c8db33781a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261528} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbe8614-b318-5962-bad4-61b3cb58bbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261819} {:answer/id #uuid "fd16fd7d-6e48-5c28-b4cd-